4.4  IOPS, throughput and latency: how fast is a disk?

TL;DR
  - Three numbers describe a disk: latency (time for one request), IOPS
    (requests per second) and throughput (bytes per second). Throughput = IOPS ×
    request size.
  - Small random requests show IOPS and latency; big sequential ones show
    throughput. A disk can be great at one and poor at the other.
  - Measure your own pattern: fio reproduces it, iostat -x shows what a running
    system does.

      iostat -x 1          # r_await/w_await: ms per request; aqu-sz: queue
      sudo iotop -o        # who is doing I/O now
      fio --name=t --rw=randread --bs=4k --size=1G --direct=1 --iodepth=32

On your machine
  nvme0n1   read 94.8 GiB in 2.8 million requests, 0.15 ms each; wrote 217.8 GiB
            in 5.1 million requests, 0.46 ms each; busy 1% of the time; 345,678
            cache flushes
  sda       read 396.7 MiB in 3,120 requests, 7.52 ms each; wrote 102.5 MiB in
            812 requests, 121.63 ms each; busy under 1% of the time
  Since     boot, 3 days, 4 h ago
  Pending   1.5 MiB not yet written, 0 B being written; data waits up to 30 s in
            RAM

Pitfalls
  - %util in iostat is the share of time with any request in flight. An NVMe
    drive at 100 % may still have plenty of capacity left.
  - dd with no oflag=direct or conv=fsync measures the page cache, not the disk.
  - A process stuck in state D with high wa in top is waiting for the disk, not
    using the CPU (16.1).
  - The fio command above creates a 1 GiB test file in the current folder: run
    it on the disk you want to test, and delete the file after.

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with atfq 4.4 --why

Related
  Commands  iostat, fio, iotop
  Paths     /proc/diskstats
  Hub       atfq disk Disk
  See also  atfq 4.1 Hard drives, SSDs and NVMe
  See also  atfq 16.1 Process states and zombies
